course: dictionary
lesson: 16030
title: Ventriloquism: a voice from somewhere else
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Ventriloquism is the skill of speaking without moving your lips, so the voice seems to come from somewhere else, usually a puppet or dummy on your knee. Read the words around it, which is its context: «The ventriloquist talked to her puppet, but her lips never moved.» The clue is "lips never moved", so the words must be coming from another place.
explain: Ventriloquism is a noun, and it is uncountable. You say «Ventriloquism is hard to learn», not «ventriloquisms». The person who does it is called a ventriloquist, and the puppet is often called a dummy.
explain: The word comes from Latin: venter means belly, and loqui means to speak. Long ago people thought the voice came from the belly. Today we know it is a trick of speaking and of how we listen, so the voice seems to come from the puppet.
fact: Ventriloquism is a noun, and it is uncountable, so you do not say "a ventriloquism" or "ventriloquisms".
fact: A ventriloquism act or show often uses a puppet or dummy sitting on the performer's knee.
fact: «The dummy seemed to talk all by itself, but the ventriloquist's lips never moved.»
fact: In science lessons, ventriloquism shows how our brains guess where a sound comes from by what we see.
fact: The word comes from Latin: venter means belly, and loqui means to speak.
exercise: «The ventriloquist talked to her dummy, but her lips did not move.» What is ventriloquism? | Drawing faces on puppets | Singing very loudly in a choir | A kind of dance | *The skill of speaking without moving your lips
exercise: The word ventriloquism is a what? | adjective | verb | *noun | adverb
exercise: «At the talent show, Ella's ventriloquism act made the whole crowd laugh.» In ventriloquism, where does the voice seem to come from? | *From somewhere else, such as a puppet | From the sky | From the speaker's lips | From the floor
test: Which of these is a ventriloquism act? | A man juggling three balls | A boy reading quietly in silence | A girl painting a picture | *A woman whose puppet talks while her own lips stay still
test: Why does a ventriloquist's puppet seem to talk? | *Because the voice seems to come from the puppet, not from the speaker | Because it has a battery inside | Because the puppet is alive | Because the stage lights are on
exam: What does ventriloquism show in a science lesson? | How the heart pumps blood | How plants grow in the sun | How ships float on water | *How our brains guess where a sound comes from
